00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.609
I'm also maintaining other projects like Pillar

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:39.729
and like 10 or 20 other patches. And it's been

00:00:39.729 --> 00:00:41.549
interesting there to kind of improve the release

00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:45.030
process. In 3 .10, I think I reverted the same

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:51.130
thing six times. What does a Python release manager

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.159
actually do? This is a conversation I had with

00:00:54.159 --> 00:00:57.179
three Python core developers, Vukas Langa, Pablo

00:00:57.179 --> 00:01:00.500
Galindo Salgado, and Hugo Van Kemenade. Vukas

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.600
and Pablo have been release managers in the past,

00:01:02.679 --> 00:01:06.060
while Hugo is the current one. Vukas works at

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.579
the Python Software Foundation, Pablo at Bloomberg,

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.180
and Hugo at the Server and Tech Agency. We talked

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.519
about what really happens behind the scenes of

00:01:14.519 --> 00:01:16.780
a release, the trickiest ones they have faced,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.989
how they even got into this role, and the fun

00:01:19.989 --> 00:01:22.430
little rituals such as YouTube release party

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:27.549
they did. Okay, she was the release manager of

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:32.030
Python 3 .8, 3 .9, Pablo 3 .10, 3 .11, and Hugo

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.230
3 .14 and 3 .15. Whose release was the least

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.689
successful one? Who wants to pick this up? It

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:46.230
wasn't mine. Not yet. No, no, no. It was not

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:50.510
mine either. Okay, okay, okay. We have to establish

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.609
some rules to judge this because I destroyed

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:55.569
GitHub as part of my release process. Is that

00:01:55.569 --> 00:01:58.989
good? Is that successful? We want to hear that

00:01:58.989 --> 00:02:05.349
story. Okay, so in 3 .10, it was 3 .10, no? So

00:02:05.349 --> 00:02:09.509
in 3 .10, we were in charge of... Well, in charge.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:13.610
We renamed the master branch to main. So GitHub

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.050
has this beautiful, kind of like, super cool

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.969
process when you're supposed to just, you know,

00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:20.389
click the button, like, do it. But turns out

00:02:20.389 --> 00:02:23.110
that that do it worked for, like, you know, repos

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:24.710
with, like, 100 issues or something. But, like,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.210
we are kind of a big whale. And the moment it

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:31.469
actually is recorded, I say something like, wouldn't

00:02:31.469 --> 00:02:33.870
it be fun if this breaks GitHub? And then immediately

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:37.099
broke GitHub. And, you know, it was fun on the

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:39.719
stream because I was recording this live at the

00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:43.080
time. But then the thing I think it was not...

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.219
clear is that we actually were reaching, we were

00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:47.120
in a back channel with GitHub, and the thing

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.460
was on fire, because apparently it was some kind

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.819
of, like, Ruby queue or something that is supposed

00:02:53.819 --> 00:02:57.199
to migrate all the forks, and, like, it was too

00:02:57.199 --> 00:03:00.060
much, and it was destroying the thing, and, like,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.580
actually all the repos were 500 because of the

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.659
queue, so if someone was changing from master

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:06.259
to main at the same time, it was blowing up.

00:03:06.539 --> 00:03:09.680
So, yeah, we kind of caused a big outage. But

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.639
is that unsuccessful? I wouldn't say that. No,

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.379
it's like we stress test lots of things that

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:17.840
we like. We often find bugs in compilers when

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.219
working on CPython and we found bugs in GitHub

00:03:20.219 --> 00:03:23.159
too. So it is successful? Yeah. I would say that's

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:25.919
successful. The unsuccessful ones that we've

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:28.840
had, and I've had a bunch of those, like... uh

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.360
hugo will have a bunch of those and um pablo

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.719
also had a bunch of those is like the micro releases

00:03:35.719 --> 00:03:39.680
that we identify have something seriously wrong

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.219
with them so that happens every now and again

00:03:42.219 --> 00:03:44.039
and then you have to have like a hot fix release

00:03:44.039 --> 00:03:46.060
that is out of schedule you just have to say

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.219
whoopsie and just go ahead and do another one

00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:52.360
really quickly so There's been some of those

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.479
where we broke ABI compatibility and it's like,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.000
oh, no, no, no. We need to just release another

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.180
one. What fortunately happens more often is that

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.699
a failed release fails during the release. So

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:09.240
before it actually goes out to people at some

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.580
point in our testing, of which we have many levels,

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:14.680
we identify like this actually doesn't work so

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.579
well. We need to start over. Like we need to

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:19.649
do a fix and actually do the entire thing. again

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:23.689
um but yeah there's been a few where we identify

00:04:23.689 --> 00:04:26.930
not one not two but like three regressions and

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.009
in quick succession like we had to just like

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:31.430
do another release with those fixes so we've

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:33.509
just done this this year and did it cause any

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:39.110
delays uh like we sort of set our own schedule

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.050
like this is based on like pep 602 that says

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:44.569
like every year getting a new python in october

00:04:44.569 --> 00:04:47.949
um but the details of it like what the particular

00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:51.410
day we are doing a particular thing, like it's

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.930
up to the release manager to put in the reschedule

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.870
for a particular Python version, like 3 .14 right

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:00.810
now. And like delays would be like, you plan

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.550
for this to happen on a Monday or Tuesday and

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.350
while you kind of do it, like you're going to

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.350
do it on Thursday, maybe Friday. And those are

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.610
usually stressful days because we're not sitting

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.629
on our hands during those days, but like actually

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:16.050
trying like to fix the regression or revert it.

00:05:16.129 --> 00:05:17.910
But sometimes it's not easy to revert it because

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.079
it's already. covered by a bunch of other changes,

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.459
and so on and so on. So I wouldn't say delays,

00:05:22.519 --> 00:05:26.160
we set the time, but sometimes it is not a matter

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.639
of like automation, of which we have a lot, but

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:32.860
like actually just doing the work to allow for

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.100
a release. So you mentioned scheduling, what

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.120
are all the things that release managers actually

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.939
do behind the scenes? Hugo? Well, let's see,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.459
the kind of the main task of the release manager

00:05:44.459 --> 00:05:46.920
is to ensure that The quality of the release

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.339
is, you know, up to scratch because a lot of

00:05:49.339 --> 00:05:52.339
people use Python and depend on it. So we don't

00:05:52.339 --> 00:05:54.360
want everything to suddenly break for people

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.439
in a bad way. So that's kind of the main thing

00:05:58.439 --> 00:06:02.139
is to like check that there's no, you know, bad

00:06:02.139 --> 00:06:05.560
things going in there. Has it changed throughout

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.339
the years? What was the difference within like

00:06:08.339 --> 00:06:11.699
release of 3 .09, for example, and between the

00:06:11.699 --> 00:06:14.709
release nowadays? or any improvements? Yes, yes.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:17.089
So when I started to be a release manager, I

00:06:17.089 --> 00:06:20.610
inherited this horrible state of the art from

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:25.889
Wookie Slanga. Because basically the releases

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:28.850
are... Well, now I don't know. Maybe it's better

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.410
with the time. But there is a big pep, I think

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.769
it's Pep 101. Yeah, Pep 101. So Pep 101 that

00:06:34.769 --> 00:06:37.189
was initially written by Larry Hastings, if I'm

00:06:37.189 --> 00:06:40.230
not incorrect. I think Barry. Or Barry, well...

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.759
They all guard. So that basically has all the

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:47.220
steps that you need to write. It's like a recipe.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.459
It's kind of a bit weird as well because I think

00:06:50.459 --> 00:06:52.839
it was Larry who left a bunch of like, stop!

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.779
Stop now! The idea is that you could search for

00:06:55.779 --> 00:06:59.240
those points. So it was very bespoke. And when

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.579
I arrived there and I did it once for the first

00:07:02.579 --> 00:07:04.660
alpha maybe, I said, I'm not doing this by hand.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.319
This is horrible. So I created this script called

00:07:08.319 --> 00:07:11.410
run -release. uh that was like automating this

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.269
and it's kind of like it's not i mean it's a

00:07:13.269 --> 00:07:15.230
it's an interesting exercise on devops because

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.269
it's not that easy to automate like you need

00:07:18.269 --> 00:07:20.730
to ssh to servers you need to copy things from

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.730
the servers you need to run things on the servers

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:26.910
also like it's very likely that your process

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:31.149
fails because random stuff. So you don't want

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:32.750
to restart from the start just because your script

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.629
crashes in the middle. So there is checkpoints.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.110
So I try to automate as much as possible. And

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.949
after that, I think the rest of it, like Thomas

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:43.230
and Hugo, have been improving that. And now we

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.430
actually run on GitHub Actions most of the process

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:48.350
because that was something that Seth did, our

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:50.750
security developer in residence. And the reason

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:55.029
is because... Before, we were running most of

00:07:55.029 --> 00:07:57.750
the release on our computers, which, you know,

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.910
we like them a lot and we keep them clean and

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.449
whatnot and safe. But, you know, you could argue

00:08:02.449 --> 00:08:05.410
that, you know, you don't trust us. We are not

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.389
that trustworthy. So now at least the build part

00:08:08.389 --> 00:08:10.509
runs in GitHub Actions, which you could argue

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:14.149
that is reproducible. And, like, you know, people

00:08:14.149 --> 00:08:17.250
can see it happening and whatnot. That was actually

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.990
a surprise for me. So before I became release

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.189
manager, I looked at PEP 101 and it's a huge

00:08:23.189 --> 00:08:25.930
PEP. There's so much detail there. But then at

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:30.170
the core sprint in Brno, was it two years ago

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.889
or something? Thomas was making a release at

00:08:32.889 --> 00:08:34.649
the same time and he said, if anyone wants to

00:08:34.649 --> 00:08:37.570
see the process, just sit next to him. So I watched

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.710
it. It's like, oh, actually, it's not as bad

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:42.490
as this whole PEP 101. We should really, you

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:44.169
know, update that whole thing, but this is like,

00:08:44.269 --> 00:08:46.669
these are the manual steps if all our automation

00:08:46.669 --> 00:08:50.750
disappeared. Humanity is just destroyed and there

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.690
is no computers and people want to know how we

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.870
release Python. They have the recipe. We can

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:59.289
actually probably just truncate this pep by a

00:08:59.289 --> 00:09:04.940
lot, but not yet, because... I didn't quite trust

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:10.100
Pablo's automation for my own releases, since

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.360
those were very old. Python 3 .8 is now end of

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.279
life. It's no longer released. And Python 3 .9

00:09:17.279 --> 00:09:20.620
is going to probably see maybe one or two more

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:24.299
before it goes end of life this October. So for

00:09:24.299 --> 00:09:27.399
this, I still use the Caveman PEP and the previous

00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:30.950
scripts, of which a lot... was already sort of

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:33.870
automated, but just a particular step. There

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.730
was no checkpointing system to do the entire

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:40.129
process. And it turned out that actually massaging

00:09:40.129 --> 00:09:45.269
this large big boy release automation, that took

00:09:45.269 --> 00:09:48.669
a few releases. That took a few years to get

00:09:48.669 --> 00:09:51.409
to the point where it doesn't crash in the middle

00:09:51.409 --> 00:09:53.330
and now you're like, oh, I don't actually know

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:57.210
where to go from here. So it is great now. It

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.580
allows us to do GitHub Actions. whatever but

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.399
just so that people don't get the wrong idea

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:05.360
like yeah i do have my own sort of like pristine

